1
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,882
( phone rings )

2
00:00:11,303 --> 00:00:13,138
( hoarsely ):
Hello?

3
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:15,140
( coughs )

4
00:00:15,182 --> 00:00:17,601
( hoarsely ):
Hello?

5
00:00:17,643 --> 00:00:19,353
I love my new job!

6
00:00:19,394 --> 00:00:20,395
Honey, you're screaming.

7
00:00:20,437 --> 00:00:22,147
You bet your ass I am!

8
00:00:22,189 --> 00:00:24,525
I just had the best
first day ever.

9
00:00:24,566 --> 00:00:26,694
The kitchen? Twice as big
as Allesandro's.

10
00:00:26,735 --> 00:00:27,695
Oh, that's great.

11
00:00:27,736 --> 00:00:28,863
Yeah, and clean?

12
00:00:28,904 --> 00:00:31,740
Not just
Health Department clean

13
00:00:31,782 --> 00:00:33,241
Monica clean!

14
00:00:33,283 --> 00:00:35,035
Awesome.

15
00:00:35,076 --> 00:00:36,912
Oh, and the people are so nice.

16
00:00:36,954 --> 00:00:38,831
There's this one guy, Jeffrey--

17
00:00:38,873 --> 00:00:40,040
he's the maitre d'.

18
00:00:40,081 --> 00:00:42,334
Chandler, you will love him.

19
00:00:42,376 --> 00:00:45,253
He is without a doubt the
funniest guy I have ever met.

20
00:00:48,423 --> 00:00:52,135
Captioning sponsored by
NBC

21
00:00:52,177 --> 00:00:54,555
and WARNER BROS. TELEVISION

22
00:00:54,596 --> 00:00:58,767
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

23
00:00:58,809 --> 00:01:01,144
♪ Your job's a joke,
you're broke ♪

24
00:01:01,186 --> 00:01:03,647
♪ Your love life's D.O.A. ♪

25
00:01:03,689 --> 00:01:08,527
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

26
00:01:08,569 --> 00:01:10,529
♪ When it hasn't been
your day, your week ♪

27
00:01:10,571 --> 00:01:13,114
♪ Your month,
or even your year ♪

28
00:01:13,156 --> 00:01:17,202
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

29
00:01:17,244 --> 00:01:19,621
♪ When the rain starts to pour ♪

30
00:01:19,663 --> 00:01:22,082
♪ I'll be there for you ♪

31
00:01:22,123 --> 00:01:24,626
♪ Like I've been there before ♪

32
00:01:24,668 --> 00:01:27,128
♪ I'll be there for you ♪

33
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:30,966
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me, too. ♪

34
00:01:40,392 --> 00:01:41,602
This is nice.

35
00:01:41,643 --> 00:01:43,020
I know.

36
00:01:51,862 --> 00:01:53,655
You need both hands
for that?

37
00:01:53,697 --> 00:01:54,949
Yeah, I kind of do.

38
00:01:54,990 --> 00:01:56,658
Well, how's this?

39
00:02:02,873 --> 00:02:04,499
Yeah?

40
00:02:11,381 --> 00:02:14,802
Well, look at you two,
holding hands.

41
00:02:14,843 --> 00:02:17,054
Huh? Is this getting serious?

42
00:02:17,096 --> 00:02:18,680
( both stammering )

43
00:02:18,722 --> 00:02:20,933
Have you not talked
about it yet?

44
00:02:22,559 --> 00:02:24,728
Am I making you uncomfortable?!

45
00:02:26,563 --> 00:02:29,024
If you were bigger,
you'd hit me, huh?

46
00:02:29,066 --> 00:02:32,820
Ah!

47
00:02:32,861 --> 00:02:34,029
I'm sorry.

48
00:02:34,071 --> 00:02:36,531
So sorry. It's obviously
way too early

49
00:02:36,573 --> 00:02:39,200
for us to be having
that conversation.

50
00:02:39,242 --> 00:02:39,910
Is it?

51
00:02:39,952 --> 00:02:41,120
Maybe not. Is it?

52
00:02:41,161 --> 00:02:42,746
Okay

53
00:02:42,788 --> 00:02:44,790
when I got divorced,
I-I didn't think
I'd feel this way

54
00:02:44,832 --> 00:02:46,541
about someone for
a really long time.

55
00:02:46,583 --> 00:02:49,294
Mm-hmm.
Then again, I didn't think
I'd meet someone like you

56
00:02:49,336 --> 00:02:51,880
and... this may
be crazy soon

57
00:02:51,922 --> 00:02:53,214
but I want you to have this.

58
00:02:55,717 --> 00:02:58,595
No, not... that's gum.

59
00:02:58,637 --> 00:03:00,931
Oh, five bucks.

60
00:03:00,973 --> 00:03:02,599
I love it when that happens,
you know?

61
00:03:02,641 --> 00:03:04,267
It's like you don't know
it's there...

62
00:03:04,309 --> 00:03:05,560
I know, Mike.
Why don't you keep digging?

63
00:03:05,602 --> 00:03:06,937
Oh, sorry.

64
00:03:10,273 --> 00:03:12,442
Oh, it's a key.

65
00:03:12,484 --> 00:03:16,195
To be honest, I think
I'd prefer the five dollars.

66
00:03:16,237 --> 00:03:18,157
It's to my apartment.

67
00:03:18,197 --> 00:03:20,408
Oh, wow. Ooh.

68
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:22,995
Big step for Phoebe and Mike.

69
00:03:23,037 --> 00:03:24,412
Yeah, look, and I don't want
you to feel like

70
00:03:24,454 --> 00:03:25,831
you have to give me your key
just because...

71
00:03:25,873 --> 00:03:26,623
Oh, no, I want to.

72
00:03:26,665 --> 00:03:29,084
Oh, thank God.

73
00:03:29,126 --> 00:03:31,586
Yeah. Ooh. Wow.

74
00:03:31,628 --> 00:03:33,839
I was starting to think
that I'd never meet someone

75
00:03:33,881 --> 00:03:36,758
that, you know, I wanted to...
do this with.

76
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:39,344
Here you go.

77
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:42,097
This is cool, huh?

78
00:03:42,139 --> 00:03:43,264
It really is.

79
00:03:43,306 --> 00:03:44,432
Oh, I know it.

80
00:03:44,474 --> 00:03:45,684
It is amazing.

81
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:47,435
These little things
open doors.

82
00:03:52,273 --> 00:03:55,443
So, I don't go back to work
for another four weeks

83
00:03:55,485 --> 00:03:57,404
but we would like our nanny
to start right away

84
00:03:57,445 --> 00:03:59,280
so that Emma could get a chance
to know her.

85
00:03:59,322 --> 00:04:01,282
I think that's really smart.

86
00:04:01,324 --> 00:04:03,202
The easier we can make
the transition

87
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:04,036
for her, the better.

88
00:04:04,078 --> 00:04:05,244
That's great! Great.

89
00:04:05,286 --> 00:04:06,621
So do you have
any questions for us?

90
00:04:06,663 --> 00:04:08,498
Not really.
All right.

91
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:10,125
Well, thank you so much
for coming.

92
00:04:10,167 --> 00:04:11,376
Thank you.

93
00:04:11,418 --> 00:04:12,711
Really nice
to meet you

94
00:04:12,753 --> 00:04:14,004
and we will call you.

95
00:04:14,046 --> 00:04:15,714
Oh, you know, wait.

96
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:17,549
I do have one question.

97
00:04:17,591 --> 00:04:20,844
Do you guys do
random drug testing?

98
00:04:22,763 --> 00:04:23,847
Boy, we, uh...

99
00:04:23,889 --> 00:04:25,306
hadn't really thought
of that.

100
00:04:25,348 --> 00:04:26,433
That's cool.

101
00:04:26,474 --> 00:04:27,517
But if you do

102
00:04:27,559 --> 00:04:30,020
I'm gonna need
three days notice.

103
00:04:30,062 --> 00:04:32,106
Okeydokey!

104
00:04:32,147 --> 00:04:34,691
Wow. We're never going
to find a nanny.

105
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:35,692
Oh, come on, Rach.

106
00:04:35,734 --> 00:04:36,735
We will. I promise.

107
00:04:36,777 --> 00:04:37,527
We have more interviews.

108
00:04:37,569 --> 00:04:38,528
Oh...

109
00:04:38,570 --> 00:04:39,988
And, worse comes to worst

110
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:42,908
we can always reconsider
the first one we met with.

111
00:04:42,950 --> 00:04:44,492
What, the blond
with no bra?!

112
00:04:44,534 --> 00:04:46,494
She was blond?

113
00:04:47,704 --> 00:04:49,706
( knocking on door )

114
00:04:49,748 --> 00:04:50,498
Just a sec!

115
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:51,583
Okay, okay.

116
00:04:51,625 --> 00:04:53,710
This one's name is Sandy.

117
00:04:53,752 --> 00:04:57,296
Okay.
She's got a degree
in Early Childhood Education.

118
00:04:57,338 --> 00:04:59,591
Uh, she worked for
her last family

119
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:00,675
for three years.

120
00:05:00,717 --> 00:05:02,052
Okay.

121
00:05:03,762 --> 00:05:05,973
Hi. I'm Sandy.

122
00:05:10,144 --> 00:05:13,063
And she's a little mannish.

123
00:05:16,608 --> 00:05:17,859
( knocking on door )

124
00:05:19,194 --> 00:05:20,195
( gasps )

125
00:05:20,237 --> 00:05:22,072
Oh, my God! David!

126
00:05:22,114 --> 00:05:25,366
Hi. I-Is this a bad time?

127
00:05:25,408 --> 00:05:27,702
No, it's a great
time. Come in!

128
00:05:27,744 --> 00:05:29,997
Wow! Hi!

129
00:05:30,038 --> 00:05:30,998
Oh, my gosh!

130
00:05:31,039 --> 00:05:32,166
What are you doing here?

131
00:05:32,207 --> 00:05:33,374
Are you back from Minsk?

132
00:05:33,416 --> 00:05:35,127
Well, just for
a couple of days.

133
00:05:35,169 --> 00:05:36,335
I'm here to explain

134
00:05:36,377 --> 00:05:38,463
to the people who gave
us our grant

135
00:05:38,505 --> 00:05:42,050
why it's a positive thing
that we've spent all their money

136
00:05:42,092 --> 00:05:44,636
and, uh... uh,
accomplished, uh, nothing.

137
00:05:44,678 --> 00:05:46,471
Who cares?
It got you here!

138
00:05:46,513 --> 00:05:48,307
Well, it got me
to New York, anyway

139
00:05:48,347 --> 00:05:50,475
and then I got in a cab
at the airport

140
00:05:50,517 --> 00:05:52,186
and the guy said, "Where to?"

141
00:05:52,227 --> 00:05:54,229
and I just... gave him
your address.

142
00:05:54,271 --> 00:05:55,981
I-I didn't even
think about it.

143
00:05:56,023 --> 00:05:59,442
Wow. Where's your luggage.

144
00:06:01,236 --> 00:06:03,113
Damn it.

145
00:06:03,155 --> 00:06:06,407
All right. So I'll call
the cab company.

146
00:06:06,449 --> 00:06:08,076
W-Wait. We can
call them later.

147
00:06:08,118 --> 00:06:10,704
Can I just... you just
stand there for a moment?

148
00:06:10,745 --> 00:06:12,497
( laughs nervously )

149
00:06:12,539 --> 00:06:16,335
Boy. Y-You... There's an
old Russian expression.

150
00:06:16,375 --> 00:06:17,794
Um, it goes...

151
00:06:17,836 --> 00:06:21,131
Shtoa yazdeez vizshu: oi.

152
00:06:21,173 --> 00:06:24,218
Um, roughly translated,
that means, um

153
00:06:24,259 --> 00:06:26,928
"This thing I'm looking at:
wow."

154
00:06:28,429 --> 00:06:29,681
Thank you.

155
00:06:29,723 --> 00:06:31,725
God, no,
you should see me when...

156
00:06:31,766 --> 00:06:34,228
Oh, actually, no,
I look pretty good.

157
00:06:34,269 --> 00:06:35,812
Are... A-Are you kidding?

158
00:06:35,854 --> 00:06:38,357
You know when you don't see
someone for a long time

159
00:06:38,397 --> 00:06:40,275
and you kind of build
them up in your head

160
00:06:40,317 --> 00:06:43,402
and then you start thinking,
"Come on, don't be crazy.

161
00:06:43,444 --> 00:06:45,155
Nobody's that beautiful."

162
00:06:45,197 --> 00:06:46,614
But... well, you are.

163
00:06:47,866 --> 00:06:48,909
Ooh.

164
00:06:48,950 --> 00:06:50,244
Well, so, uh...

165
00:06:50,285 --> 00:06:52,787
well, are you seeing anyone?

166
00:06:53,955 --> 00:06:56,583
No.

167
00:06:56,624 --> 00:06:59,627
I'm just... I'm the
worst person ever!

168
00:06:59,669 --> 00:07:02,339
How can I not tell David
that I'm seeing Mike?!

169
00:07:02,381 --> 00:07:04,174
Maybe he didn't give you
a chance.

170
00:07:04,216 --> 00:07:06,051
He said,
"Are you seeing someone?"

171
00:07:06,093 --> 00:07:07,886
And I said, "No."

172
00:07:07,928 --> 00:07:10,680
Oh, well, that would've been
your window.

173
00:07:10,722 --> 00:07:11,848
Yeah. I mean

174
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:13,641
I don't know, I was looking...

175
00:07:13,683 --> 00:07:16,728
I was looking in his eyes,
and I was just thinking

176
00:07:16,770 --> 00:07:18,897
"Oh, my God, it's David.
David's here."

177
00:07:18,939 --> 00:07:22,109
He's just,
he's so irresistible.

178
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:22,817
Really?

179
00:07:22,859 --> 00:07:23,985
The scientist guy?

180
00:07:24,027 --> 00:07:25,528
Really? Chandler?

181
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:33,787
Continue.

182
00:07:33,828 --> 00:07:35,372
Okay, then it gets worse.

183
00:07:35,414 --> 00:07:36,539
'Cause then I told him

184
00:07:36,581 --> 00:07:38,333
that I would see him
tomorrow night.

185
00:07:38,375 --> 00:07:39,042
Phoebe!

186
00:07:39,084 --> 00:07:41,836
I know! Evil!

187
00:07:41,878 --> 00:07:43,338
And-And-And...

188
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:45,632
I like Mike so much, you know.

189
00:07:45,673 --> 00:07:47,801
It's just going really well.

190
00:07:47,842 --> 00:07:49,803
Oh, my God!

191
00:07:49,844 --> 00:07:52,222
Wow. Isn't it ironic
that David would show up

192
00:07:52,264 --> 00:07:54,849
on the same day that
you and Mike exchange keys?

193
00:07:54,891 --> 00:07:57,269
Uh-huh.

194
00:07:57,311 --> 00:08:00,814
Yeah, you know, and given
my lifelong search for irony

195
00:08:00,855 --> 00:08:02,899
you can imagine how happy I am.

196
00:08:04,359 --> 00:08:06,027
What are you going to do?

197
00:08:06,069 --> 00:08:09,281
I mean, I guess I just have
to... tell David

198
00:08:09,323 --> 00:08:11,866
that nothing can happen
between us.

199
00:08:11,908 --> 00:08:13,618
Unless I don't.

200
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:18,539
You know, complicated moral
situation-- no right, no wrong.

201
00:08:18,581 --> 00:08:19,624
You have to tell David.

202
00:08:19,666 --> 00:08:21,042
Okay, I knew

203
00:08:21,084 --> 00:08:23,128
I should've had this
conversation with Joey.

204
00:08:24,921 --> 00:08:28,008
Funniest guy she's ever met.

205
00:08:28,049 --> 00:08:30,427
I'm funny, right?

206
00:08:30,469 --> 00:08:34,014
What do you know?
You're a door.

207
00:08:34,055 --> 00:08:36,266
You just like
"knock-knock" jokes.

208
00:08:39,144 --> 00:08:42,147
Save it for inside.

209
00:08:43,106 --> 00:08:44,524
Hey!
Hey!
Hey!

210
00:08:44,565 --> 00:08:46,526
So, Oklahoma's a crazy place.

211
00:08:46,567 --> 00:08:49,237
You know, they call it
the Sooner State.

212
00:08:49,279 --> 00:08:52,115
Frankly, I'd sooner be
in any other state.

213
00:08:53,950 --> 00:08:56,870
And what's with Oklahoma having
a panhandle?

214
00:08:56,911 --> 00:08:59,247
Can all states have stuff
like that?

215
00:08:59,289 --> 00:09:02,917
"Hey, yeah, I'm from the, uh,
waistband of Wyoming...

216
00:09:02,959 --> 00:09:06,421
But when I was seven,
we headed over to the crotch."

217
00:09:06,463 --> 00:09:09,757
Was your cabin pressurized?

218
00:09:09,799 --> 00:09:10,758
( chuckles )

219
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:12,135
And don't get me started

220
00:09:12,177 --> 00:09:14,095
on the way the people
from Tulsa talk.

221
00:09:14,137 --> 00:09:15,472
Okay.

222
00:09:16,973 --> 00:09:18,392
What's with the word "y'all"?

223
00:09:18,433 --> 00:09:19,600
You know, just, two words

224
00:09:19,642 --> 00:09:21,395
just pushed together?

225
00:09:21,436 --> 00:09:23,313
Are we all allowed
to do that?

226
00:09:23,355 --> 00:09:25,690
Because if so,
I say why stop there?

227
00:09:25,732 --> 00:09:29,319
You know, your-your
"new poodle" could
be your "noodle."

228
00:09:29,361 --> 00:09:31,946
And "fried chicken,"
could be "fricken."

229
00:09:31,988 --> 00:09:35,409
Waiter! Waiter, excuse me.
"I'll have the fricken."

230
00:09:38,245 --> 00:09:40,080
See, that's, that's funny
with the fricken, right?

231
00:09:40,121 --> 00:09:42,290
No, it just reminded me
of something

232
00:09:42,332 --> 00:09:43,958
this guy did today at work.

233
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,087
I-I told you about that
funny guy, Jeffrey, right?

234
00:09:47,128 --> 00:09:49,172
Yeah, he came up.

235
00:09:49,214 --> 00:09:51,132
Well, he did this bit...

236
00:09:51,174 --> 00:09:55,762
You probably had to be there,
but it was, uh, Liza Minelli

237
00:09:55,803 --> 00:09:59,266
locked in our freezer,
eating a raw chicken.

238
00:09:59,307 --> 00:10:00,016
Oh!

239
00:10:00,058 --> 00:10:02,727
( laughing )

240
00:10:02,769 --> 00:10:04,896
Were you there?

241
00:10:04,938 --> 00:10:07,524
No, but it sounds
like it was fricken funny.

242
00:10:10,527 --> 00:10:12,904
I really do understand
how hard it's got to be

243
00:10:12,946 --> 00:10:14,448
to leave your child
with another person.

244
00:10:14,489 --> 00:10:17,992
I mean... it's like leaving
behind a piece of your heart.

245
00:10:18,034 --> 00:10:21,496
Sandy, that's exactly
what it is.

246
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:25,792
Are you gay?

247
00:10:25,833 --> 00:10:27,294
Ross!

248
00:10:27,335 --> 00:10:29,629
It's okay. I get that a lot
doing what I do.

249
00:10:29,670 --> 00:10:30,755
But I am straight.

250
00:10:30,797 --> 00:10:32,132
I'm engaged, actually.

251
00:10:32,173 --> 00:10:33,508
Her name's Delia.

252
00:10:33,550 --> 00:10:35,552
Oh, that's pretty.

253
00:10:36,928 --> 00:10:39,514
So you're-you're just...
like, a...

254
00:10:39,556 --> 00:10:41,600
guy who's a nanny?

255
00:10:43,393 --> 00:10:46,687
I realize how it's a bit
unorthodox for some people

256
00:10:46,729 --> 00:10:48,523
but I really believe

257
00:10:48,565 --> 00:10:51,485
the most satisfying thing
you can do with your life

258
00:10:51,526 --> 00:10:52,902
is take care of a child.

259
00:10:52,944 --> 00:10:55,280
Okay.

260
00:10:57,365 --> 00:10:58,825
Like at my last job

261
00:10:58,866 --> 00:11:01,035
I met Daniel
when he was three weeks old

262
00:11:01,077 --> 00:11:04,539
and I got to watch him grow
into this awesome person.

263
00:11:04,581 --> 00:11:08,168
When I left, I said,
